WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
Synthesis and breakdown set the cAMP level.

2
00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:10.440
Once that messenger activates PKA, why do heart and fat cells respond differently?

3
00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:15.520
The messenger is common, but the reachable machinery need not be.

4
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.680
That is the puzzle.

5
00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:27.683
At low cAMP, PKA rests as paired regulatory and catalytic subunits.

6
00:00:29.103 --> 00:00:31.803
Each catalytic subunit is a complete kinase.

7
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:36.803
Each regulatory partner keeps one catalytic site inactive.

8
00:00:38.183 --> 00:00:42.163
The regulatory pair acts as an off switch until messenger arrives.

9
00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:48.027
A kinase modifies a target protein by transferring phosphate from ATP.

10
00:00:49.407 --> 00:00:51.067
The target must enter a groove.

11
00:00:52.327 --> 00:00:56.787
A regulatory segment mimics a target and occupies that groove first.

12
00:00:58.107 --> 00:01:01.707
Blocking the groove prevents genuine targets from receiving phosphate.

13
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.170
cAMP binds the regulatory subunits and changes their shape.

14
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.530
Their blocking segments release the grooves.

15
00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:15.070
In the classical model, catalytic subunits come away free.

16
00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:21.430
Evidence suggests anchored complexes can act nearby without fully coming apart.

17
00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:26.930
Either picture exposes catalytic activity to targets within reach.

18
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.040
Now place the same active PKA across both cells.

19
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.520
Both cells receive the same messenger level and display the same kinase.

20
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:43.040
One cell offers green target strips, while the other offers different strips.

21
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.700
Must their responses match?

22
00:01:51.633 --> 00:01:57.713
No. An active PKA can modify only compatible targets it reaches.

23
00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:04.013
Different cells make different target proteins, so identical messenger and kinase

24
00:02:04.013 --> 00:02:07.093
activity can produce different cellular actions.

25
00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:12.693
The response follows the proteins present, not a special kind of cAMP.

26
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:19.227
In fat, reachable targets include hormone-sensitive lipase and perilipin, supporting

27
00:02:19.227 --> 00:02:25.927
fat breakdown. In heart, other targets alter calcium entry, cycling, contraction,

28
00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:32.627
and relaxation. Liver offers another set, including enzymes that control glycogen.

29
00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:36.267
The relay is shared; the target set differs.

30
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:39.890
Reach also matters within one cell.

31
00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:46.090
An anchoring protein can hold PKA beside a selected substrate.

32
00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:50.830
The address narrows which target can meet the active kinase.

33
00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:57.130
Nearby cyclase supplies cAMP, while nearby phosphodiesterase removes it.

34
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:06.763
Experiments reveal local cAMP regions and receptor-specific PKA activity.

35
00:03:08.383 --> 00:03:12.743
Local production, local removal, binding, and restricted movement may all

36
00:03:12.743 --> 00:03:17.063
contribute. Their importance varies and remains debated.

37
00:03:18.283 --> 00:03:22.243
The local patterns are observed; the mechanism is not fully settled.

38
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:28.513
Activation therefore opens the catalytic groove, but access gives the signal

39
00:03:28.513 --> 00:03:34.773
meaning. The text now tests that groove with competition and examines the evidence

40
00:03:34.773 --> 00:03:35.773
for local signalling.

41
00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:40.713
Those experiments separate what is established from what is proposed.
